<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
        There is no INTERNET permission here and there never will be. It is stated
        to users as a fact their phone enforces, so it is checked rather than
        trusted: verifyNoInternetPermission fails the build if the merged manifest
        ever contains one, whether it came from here or from a dependency.

        WorkManager declares ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE so that callers can schedule work
        behind a network constraint. This app schedules two pieces of work, a Pulse
        reminder and a widget refresh, and neither has a network constraint because
        neither could use one. The permission is removed rather than shipped, since
        a permission the app cannot make use of still shows up in the app info
        screen a person is invited to go and check.

        **Phase 6 is the first real caller, and the removal was checked against the
        library rather than assumed.** In work-runtime 2.11.2,
        WorkConstraintsTracker.track filters its controllers by
        ConstraintController.hasConstraint for the spec it is given, so a request
        with no constraints never reaches the network tracker and never starts it.
        Everything that does touch ConnectivityManager, the state read and the
        default network callback registration, catches SecurityException already. A
        constraint free daily reminder is therefore safe without the permission.
        **Adding any constraint to a work request in this app means putting the
        permission back**, which would be a change to what the privacy policy invites
        people to verify, so it is a decision for the owner and not a build fix.

        The three WorkManager permissions this app does ship are WAKE_LOCK,
        RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED and FOREGROUND_SERVICE. None is a network permission
        and verifyNoInternetPermission passes on both variants. MASTER_BUILD_PROMPT
        section 18 puts every permission beyond notifications out of scope for v1,
        which these are, and docs/BUILD_STATE.md records the question as the
        builder's call. Phase 6 does not settle it: it uses WorkManager because
        section 3 and 12.1 both require it for this reminder, and reports what the
        merged manifest gains.
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE"
        tools:node="remove" />

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!--
        The focus session Live Update, MASTER_BUILD_PROMPT 14b.6 and design-v3.md
        11.4. `Notification.ProgressStyle` is a promoted ongoing notification and
        the platform gates promotion on this permission.

        **It is a notifications permission and not a network one**, which is what
        keeps it inside what MASTER_BUILD_PROMPT 18 allows and what keeps
        verifyNoInternetPermission green on both variants: that gate matches five
        exact permission names, INTERNET, ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE, ACCESS_WIFI_STATE,
        CHANGE_NETWORK_STATE and CHANGE_WIFI_STATE, and this is none of them.

        A normal permission, so it is granted at install and shows no prompt. It
        grants nothing beyond asking the system to promote a notification the app is
        already allowed to post, and the system may still refuse:
        NotificationManagerCompat.canPostPromotedNotifications is checked before
        every post and a false answer degrades to the ongoing notification with no
        word to the user, per design-v3.md 11.4.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_PROMOTED_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!--
        Required by design-v3.md section 9, which specifies sixteen haptic events.
        A normal permission: it is granted at install, shows no prompt, and gives
        access to nothing but the vibrator on this device.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.VIBRATE" />

    <application
        android:name=".ClarityApp"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="${appLabel}"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="false"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.ClarityNow"
        tools:targetApi="31">

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|screenLayout|keyboardHidden|uiMode"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.ClarityNow"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
            The two Live Update actions, `Add 10 min` and `End`, and the dismissal of
            the ongoing notification. Addendum 01 5c requires both actions to work
            without opening the app, so they arrive here rather than at an Activity.

            **Not exported.** Every intent that reaches it is built by this app with
            an explicit component, so there is no filter to match and nothing outside
            the app can start a session, end one or add time to one.

            It writes through ClarityRepository like every other writer in the app,
            per CLAUDE.md rule on the single write path. It is not a second one.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".notifications.FocusActionReceiver"
            android:exported="false" />
    </application>
</manifest>
